WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:11.840
Chief Executive’s Report

00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:20.340
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Since we last met, we have
held the NHS Expo in Manchester, and I want

00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:27.029
to thank Simon Enright and team for putting
on a fabulous event that, as I said at the

00:00:27.029 --> 00:00:32.470
time, brought together the can-do crowd at
no expense to the taxpayer, but demonstrating

00:00:32.470 --> 00:00:38.050
really significant change happening in many
parts of the NHS. I have also had the opportunity

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:43.370
to speak to the Queen’s Nursing Institute,
to the leaders of the Ambulance Trust, the

00:00:43.370 --> 00:00:47.621
Health Service General Summit, and one of
my very regular appearances at the Public

00:00:47.621 --> 00:00:53.600
Accounts Committee, where I had the opportunity
to remind Parliament about the funding outlook

00:00:53.600 --> 00:01:01.469
for the National Health Service and answer
the questions about the financial position

00:01:01.469 --> 00:01:05.480
this year and in five years’ time, and make
the point that in the intervening years – next

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:10.180
year, the year after, the year after that
– we did not get fully what we asked for,

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:14.100
but nevertheless, as part of the two-year
planning round that we are now embarked on

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:19.200
and the STP process, we will be exemplifying
the sorts of changes that are required in

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:25.229
order to live within the funding that has
been allocated. There will be a further opportunity

00:01:25.229 --> 00:01:30.409
to explore some of those themes on 18th October,
when I will be before the Health Select Committee

00:01:30.409 --> 00:01:36.180
discussing identical matters, and then the
House of Lords having an equivalent hearing,

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:42.560
with a further opportunity later on before
Christmas.

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:46.060
The subject in hand actually of the Public
Accounts Committee was the collapse of the

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:51.180
Uniting Care contract in Cambridgeshire, and
I think that pointed to some serious disconnects

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:56.600
between decision-making locally, between different
statutory organisations, between different

00:01:56.600 --> 00:02:05.579
trusts, and the CCG, the role of external
advisors, and the supervisory regime that

00:02:05.579 --> 00:02:13.080
NHS England and NHS Improvement jointly bring
to bear on these new forms of contractual

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:20.420
arrangement. In the light of the sensible
National Audit Office report, and the two

00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:26.340
external investigations which we commissioned
– NHS England and NHSI – we will be publishing

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:34.590
by the end of October a set of clear processes
that will need to be gone through before those

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:42.920
kinds of schemes are initiated going forward.
We have also used the intervening period to

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:48.900
kick the tyres on some approaches that were
being contemplated around the country to make

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:53.480
sure that they do not go forward if those
lessons have not been learned in the meantime.

00:02:54.140 --> 00:03:00.940
I am very pleased to say that we successfully
got the planning guidance and the proposed

00:03:00.950 --> 00:03:08.800
CQUINs and the draft standard contract issued
on 22nd September which, as we will discuss

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:14.060
later on, means we have been able to bring
forward by about three months the annual planning

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:22.680
process. We are aiming to have this wrapped
up substantively by Christmas, with various

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:31.750
milestones along the way, including the 21st
October deadline for local areas to propose

00:03:31.750 --> 00:03:37.370
their sustainability and transformation plans,
the first two years of which obviously then

00:03:37.370 --> 00:03:42.849
need to be one and the same as the funding
agreements for the contracts, which I think

00:03:42.849 --> 00:03:49.360
Matthew will discuss later on in the agenda;
24th November then being submission of operational

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:54.129
plans; and 23rd December being when we want
to try and wrap this whole thing up so as

00:03:54.129 --> 00:04:00.470
to avoid Jim Mackey and myself personally
having to arbitrate any remaining contract

00:04:00.470 --> 00:04:04.010
disputes early in January.

00:04:04.010 --> 00:04:09.480
We have various other things on the agenda
over the coming three or four weeks, ahead

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:16.130
of our next meeting. I think we are making
good process on implementing our Mental Health

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:20.160
Task Force report, and Claire Murdoch and
Stephen Firn are here today to talk about

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:26.940
some of those next steps. Cally Palmer will
be publishing the one-year-on report from

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:33.570
the Cancer Task Force, and we hope to be able
to set some tangible next steps on the upgrade

00:04:33.570 --> 00:04:40.180
of radiotherapy equipment and linear accelerators
across the country, amongst other developments.

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:46.150
We are shortly going to be able to announce
investment decisions in GP premises upgrades

00:04:46.150 --> 00:04:52.670
this year right across the country, after
a good process of proposal development from

00:04:52.670 --> 00:05:00.010
CCGs, and we have some big decisions to make
in the light of the STP proposals about how

00:05:00.010 --> 00:05:06.610
we use next year’s funding for the spread
of new care models. We have items on our agenda

00:05:06.610 --> 00:05:12.930
this morning about lessons from the care homes
and the integrated hospital and primary and

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:21.750
community social care model. We would like
to make some substantial investments in some

00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:27.570
national spread of some of those; we will
be making decisions on those in the next six

00:05:27.570 --> 00:05:32.200
to eight weeks in the light of what we see
are investable propositions coming out of

00:05:32.200 --> 00:05:33.980
individual STPs.

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:41.060
Then finally, and perhaps a little bit left
field: obviously with a lot of the public

00:05:41.060 --> 00:05:45.490
focus on the National Health Service, the
kind of sturm und drang of the here and now,

00:05:45.490 --> 00:05:49.230
we sometimes do not notice some of the bigger
changes that are potentially much more profound

00:05:49.230 --> 00:05:53.510
in terms of the prospects for the nation’s
health. I think it is also just worth recording

00:05:53.510 --> 00:05:58.640
that, since we last met, the latest figures
for adult smoking in this country have been

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:03.720
published, and actually show that one&nbsp;million
fewer people are smoking now compared to five

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:08.780
years ago: 7.2&nbsp;million compared to 8.2&nbsp;million.
In terms of all the things that are likely

00:06:08.780 --> 00:06:13.580
to have an impact on the health of the nation,
looking out five or ten years, it is the smoking

00:06:13.580 --> 00:06:18.950
rate, together with action on obesity, which
is going to be as important as anything else

00:06:18.950 --> 00:06:23.590
we are going to be discussing here today.
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: The rate has come down from

00:06:23.590 --> 00:06:29.910
about 20% to 17%?
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: We need it to be in the 13–13.5%

00:06:29.910 --> 00:06:37.750
zone by 2020 in order to deliver on the 30,000
lives saved that the Cancer Task Force is

00:06:37.750 --> 00:06:42.850
supposed to be implementing. Recognising that
this is also a huge source of health inequality:

00:06:42.850 --> 00:06:48.370
probably half of the class life expectancy
difference in this country is explained by

00:06:48.370 --> 00:06:54.690
differential smoking rates.
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you, Simon. May I invite

00:06:54.690 --> 00:07:04.310
any comments or queries, to the Chief Executive?
&gt;&gt;David Roberts: Is it worth giving us your

00:07:04.310 --> 00:07:10.730
sense as to the maturity of the STP process?
Clearly a lot rides on getting those right.

00:07:10.730 --> 00:07:19.880
&gt;&gt;Simon Stevens: Yes. Obviously, we are not,
as it were, approving STPs. STPs are being

00:07:19.880 --> 00:07:28.220
developed by local health and care organisations.
We are reviewing and supporting those, and

00:07:28.220 --> 00:07:33.910
aiming to then ensure that, come 21st October,
they provide the basis on which people make

00:07:33.910 --> 00:07:38.610
their operational investment decisions for
the next year and the year after.

00:07:38.610 --> 00:07:42.190
The reality, of course, is that the scale
of the challenge in different parts of the

00:07:42.190 --> 00:07:48.650
country varies pretty substantially, and so
in practice I think what we are going to find

00:07:48.650 --> 00:07:55.520
is that there is going to be a cohort of proposals
that people want to get going on and which

00:07:55.520 --> 00:08:02.160
are well worked through, and would have our
support in late October/early November. There

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:06.500
will be others where they obviously know what
they need to do for next year and the year

00:08:06.500 --> 00:08:10.510
after, but there is a more deliberative process
required, particularly in terms of public

00:08:10.510 --> 00:08:16.000
engagement and citizen dialogue. We will talk
a bit about that in the NHS Citizens discussion

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:22.190
later, and that will take a bit longer. We
are working – we, in this case, being ourselves

00:08:22.190 --> 00:08:28.160
and NHS Improvement – with the 44 STPs to
get a good sense from them as to where along

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:36.110
that spectrum of ready‑to‑roll versus
strategic change and engagement work still

00:08:36.110 --> 00:08:42.870
required they sit. In any event, the whole
point of having the 2017/2018, 2018/2019 round

00:08:42.870 --> 00:08:48.450
brought forward is that it actually focuses
minds. This is now not a sort of speculative

00:08:48.450 --> 00:08:55.180
2020 kind of proposition; every journey of
1,000 miles begins with a single step, and

00:08:55.180 --> 00:08:58.430
the journey of the next five years begins
with, what you are going to make decisions

00:08:58.430 --> 00:09:04.410
on for 2017/2018, 2018/2019?
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you. I invite the Board

00:09:04.410 --> 00:09:06.550
to note the Chief Executive’s report.

